--TEST--
A two-case union admits neither a case outside it nor a value typed as the whole enum, at an argument, at a declaration, after an `||` of two comparisons and at the checked conversion itself (`rule:types/single-value-types`, `rule:types/narrowing`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/a-union-of-cases-is-a-narrower-type
// `rule:types/single-value-types`'s third row makes a wider value reach a narrower
// union through a guard or a checked `as`, and every line below is a way of
// arriving without one. The `||` is the one worth pinning: a single `==`
// narrows its subject to that case's own type, so a reader expects two of them
// to narrow to the pair, and they do not — the guard proves membership of a
// set the checker builds no residue for, and `as Mode::Read|Mode::Write` is
// what enters it. The last line is the other half: the conversion is proved
// impossible where the operand names one case and the set does not hold it.
enum Mode {
    Read,
    Write,
    Exec,
}

final class Open {
    public static function file(Mode::Read|Mode::Write $m): int {
        return $m as int;
    }
}

echo Open::file(Mode::Exec), "\n";

Mode $held = Mode::Read;
echo Open::file($held), "\n";

Mode $either = Mode::Write;
if ($either == Mode::Read || $either == Mode::Write) {
    echo Open::file($either), "\n";
}

Mode::Read|Mode::Write $narrow = Mode::Exec;
echo $narrow as int, "\n";

echo (Mode::Exec as Mode::Read|Mode::Write) as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Mode::Read|Mode::Write`, found `Mode`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `Mode::Read|Mode::Write`, found `Mode`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `Mode::Read|Mode::Write`, found `Mode`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `Mode::Read|Mode::Write`, found `Mode`
%A
error[E0470]: `Mode::Exec` is not one of `Mode::Read`, `Mode::Write`
%A
error: aborting due to 5 errors
